      "answer": "- The grid cannot keep pace with rising electricity demand, leading to lengthening connection queues and competition for scarce grid capacity [2].\n- New housing and industrial development compete with data centers and generation projects for grid capacity [2].\n- Service disruption may occur if left unaddressed [2].\n- Stability is a concern due to the displacement of synchronous generators by converter-connected resources [2].\n- Converter-connected resources reduce traditional stabilizing properties like inertia, voltage support, fault current contribution, and damping [2].\n- Faster-moving and harder-to-observe variables include stability, voltage support, and damping [2].\n- AI-based tools' performance depends heavily on the quality of data and models they rely on [2].\n- The grid must now cope with distributed energy resources such as rooftop solar, batteries, EVs, and flexible demand [3].\n- Large inverter-based power sources and vast data centers represent a new, more concentrated form of generation and demand [3].\n- Sharp peaks can produce challenges for voltage management, transformer loading, feeder capacity, and protection settings [3].\n- The grid is transforming from unidirectional systems to systems with dynamic two-way flows [3].\n- Voltage management, transformer loading, feeder capacity, and protection settings are new challenges created by these changes [3].\n- The grid must now manage conditions that are faster-moving and harder to observe and model than thermal limits [2].\n- The grid faces new challenges at both the distribution and transmission levels [3].\n- The grid must now manage evolving real-time conditions and instantaneous disturbances [2].\n- Evolving real-time conditions and instantaneous disturbances are uncertainties in operational planning [2].\n- Decisions in long-term planning and connection studies include what to build, defer, or connect, and where to locate (investment focus) [5].\n- Forecast error on variab