    {
      "question": "How can the cost of capital for clean energy projects in emerging and developing economies be reduced?",
      "model": "C:\\Users\\HP\\.cache\\ecorag_models\\Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct",
      "prompt_version": "v3",
      "answer": "- A 1 percentage point reduction in the cost of capital can lead to a reduction of USD 150 billion in average annual financing costs in the NZE Scenario between 2024 and 2050 [1].\n- A 2 percentage point reduction in the cost of capital could almost double this reduction to USD 300 billion per year [1].\n- Bringing down the cost of capital requires addressing risks across various parts of the energy economy, including different business models, scales of projects, and prevalent risks in different sectors [2].\n- It is essential to dig into the features of different parts of the energy sector to examine their specific elements, business models, and risks [2].\n- Risks that push up the cost of capital extend well beyond the energy sector, highlighting the need for a broad effort to create the conditions that will allow all countries to benefit from participation in the new clean energy economy [3].\n- Mobilising private finance at the scale needed will require at least a tripling in international concessional funds to help improve the risk-return profile of clean energy projects across the electricity, end-use, and low-emission-fuel sectors [5].\n- Lowering the cost of capital can substantially bring down the overall cost of transitions and reduce the costs paid by consumers [1].\n- Better risk management through strong policy frameworks and regulation as well as enhanced deployment of de-risking instruments are key [1].",
